compelling mission to me.

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:17.530
So those two things
together keep me in place,

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:19.640
helping new things happen.

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:22.910
So our platform services
team is 20 folks,

00:27:22.910 --> 00:27:27.200
and I'll tell you a little
bit more about what we are.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:31.790
We have about 110 critical
and important services.

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:33.780
I mean, we run
hundreds of services.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:36.270
But there's about 110
that are critical.

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:41.870
We have 75 distinct customer
groups, and that's complexity.

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:43.810
We're, sort of, a little
non-profit business

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:45.940
inside Stanford.

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:51.400
And we have somewhere between
650 and 700 server instances,

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:52.810
mostly virtual.

00:27:52.810 --> 00:27:55.960
We have about 120 databases.

00:27:55.960 --> 00:28:00.680
And on-prem, we manage about a
quarter of a petabyte of data.

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:03.050
So what's our problem?

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:04.700
What were we trying to do?

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:11.538
So backup and archive is
just a critical service.

00:28:11.538 --> 00:28:13.580
It's probably the most
critical service we offer.

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:16.150
We cannot lose data.

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:20.860
We have all of the patients
and researchers and faculty

00:28:20.860 --> 00:28:24.550
who depend on us to keep
the data safe and secure,

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:28.160
private and available.

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:29.980
We also with our
business model have

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:34.360
to transparently pass costs
of our archive storage

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:37.957
at Google onto our customer
groups at Stanford.

00:28:37.957 --> 00:28:40.290
And so we have to use lots
of different Google projects.

00:28:40.290 --> 00:28:42.340
That's the easiest
way for us to do that.

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:45.780
We have all the complex
backup and archive needs

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:47.520
of the large enterprise.

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:49.510
With all the virtual
machines, I mentioned,

00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:54.000
we have bare metal systems, lots
of local centralized storage

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:58.230
systems, as well as lots of
different operating systems

00:28:58.230 --> 00:29:01.650
and database technologies.

00:29:01.650 --> 00:29:06.720
We maintained an aging
tape backup solution.

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:12.390
It worked, but it
kept many people busy

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:15.630
safely and securely
handling a lot of tapes,

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:17.640
and with it we
felt like we needed

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:19.987
to do lots of full backups
all the time, which

00:29:19.987 --> 00:29:21.820
really puts a load on
our production system.

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:25.440
So it's not ideal, but we were
waiting for the right partner

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:28.680
to work with our
strategic cloud partner

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:31.700
Google to help us move forward.

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:35.180
And when Cohesity GA
at Google Next 2018,

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:39.130
we started working
with them right away.

00:29:39.130 --> 00:29:44.700
So all those folks need us to
have those dependable backups.

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:47.220
And we felt like we had
to backup the cloud.

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:50.490
With the 11 nines of durability
for the storage that Google

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:52.410
provides, that's just
better than anything

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:53.940
we could do on-prem.

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:57.150
And I would say that
both Cohesity and Google

00:29:57.150 --> 00:30:02.400
with the encryption at rest
and at transit, they were

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:03.840
meeting our security needs.

00:30:06.390 --> 00:30:12.420
So Cohesity provides
a fully modern backup

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:14.100
and archive solution.

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:18.270
We have agents where needed
with our bare metal systems,

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:20.550
but we have deep
and full integration

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:23.640
with our virtual
machine infrastructure

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:28.030
and direct interfacing with
our central storage solutions.

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:32.400
And so that's just so
many steps forward for us.

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:36.060
And Cohesity's fully
hydrated incremental backups

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:38.550
let us do full backups
much less often, much

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:41.730
less load on our
production systems.

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.310
And they're letting us use
all the Google projects

00:30:44.310 --> 00:30:46.570
we might want to use,
and it's really easy.

00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:49.500
And that's fantastic.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:54.360
The recovery of data with
Cohesity is excellent.

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:56.220
I'd say we really
like the ability

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:59.190
to immediately recover a virtual
machine from that local storage

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:05.680
while we move back to normal
operations, and the cloud

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:06.730
capability--

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:08.860
the recoveries there are
just frankly much faster

00:31:08.860 --> 00:31:10.193
than I would have ever expected.

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:14.810
We have about 3/4
of petabytes now

00:31:14.810 --> 00:31:18.410
archived on Google
Cloud and growing.

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:21.760
I also want to say that our
partnership with Cohesity,

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:22.950
I mean, I appreciate it.

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:26.540
We have a very complex, nuanced,
security infrastructure on-prem

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:29.450
with like dozens of VLANs.

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:32.570
And we've had other people we've
talked to in industry basically

00:31:32.570 --> 00:31:33.440
say, don't do that.

00:31:33.440 --> 00:31:34.250
That's complicated.

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:37.340
And we feel like
we have to, so I

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:40.410
appreciate that Cohesity's
helped us make that work.

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:43.620
So we're happy with the
solution we have now,

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:46.010
but we also look to the future.

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:50.960
So Cohesity's features and
security and multi-tenancy,

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:52.380
we are looking
forward to testing

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:54.380
letting other partners
in the School of Medicine

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:57.490
who have systems we're
not centrally managing use

00:31:57.490 --> 00:32:00.290
our cohesive system right
alongside us securely

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:02.570
and safely, and that's great.

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:06.980
And the future with possibly
using hybrid and DR solutions,

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:09.110
we've already got the
data archived out there.

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:10.890
If there was some
kind of catastrophe,

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:13.473
it would be really great to be
able to use that for a disaster

00:32:13.473 --> 00:32:14.720
recovery solution.

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:16.310
So we'll be testing that.

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:20.310
I'm also very interested in the
just announced very low cost,

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:22.760
long-term archive solution
that Google announced.

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:25.460
We're using cold
line storage, and we

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:29.990
think that is very cost
effective and performing

00:32:29.990 --> 00:32:30.990
very well for us.

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:33.530
But lower cost, we're
very sensitive to costs.

00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:36.590
So we care a lot about that, and
I appreciate that announcement.

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:37.790
That was a surprise.

