00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:07.572
So what Actifio does, I'll
give you a little spill on us

00:34:07.572 --> 00:34:09.739
and then let Matt talk for
the majority of the time.

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:12.920
But what Actifio does
is we enable enterprises

00:34:12.920 --> 00:34:16.460
to deliver enterprise
data as a service on-prem

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:19.040
or in any of the public
clouds, specifically focusing

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:20.909
on Google for Matt's use case.

00:34:20.909 --> 00:34:22.400
So what that means
is we're really

00:34:22.400 --> 00:34:25.780
focused on three key area, and
the first one is going to be

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:27.290
an incremental forever backup.

00:34:27.290 --> 00:34:29.719
So Actifio supports
a wide variety

00:34:29.719 --> 00:34:33.590
of mission critical applications
and databases, Oracle, SQL,

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:37.080
SAP Hana, MySQL
Postgres, et cetera.

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:39.139
And we're going to take
an initial ingest of all

00:34:39.139 --> 00:34:42.170
of your applications and
databases on day one.

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:45.199
Then the end user is going
to create a policy where

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:48.560
he tells us he wants just those
change walks captured every six

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:49.623
hours, for example.

00:34:49.623 --> 00:34:52.040
And then we're going to roll
those changes into our master

00:34:52.040 --> 00:34:53.060
copy.

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:55.820
And what this does is it allows
our second use case disaster

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:58.120
recovery where we
actually are allowing

00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:02.720
ATB to have our virtual Actifio
appliance in one region of GCP

00:35:02.720 --> 00:35:05.690
and replicating over to
a second region in GCP

00:35:05.690 --> 00:35:08.340
where they can actually
spin up in a disaster

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:10.040
scenario a
multi-terabyte mission

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:13.730
critical databases in a matter
of two to three minutes.

00:35:13.730 --> 00:35:16.520
So our third use case--
and it kind flows back

00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:18.650
into the initial ingest
I was talking about--

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:21.420
is that test data
management use case.

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:23.870
So Actifio was founded, and
we thought we're already

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:24.890
taking these back ups.

00:35:24.890 --> 00:35:28.530
So why don't we just reuse these
backups that we already have.

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:30.320
So what we allow you
to do is provision

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.690
out multiple copies for
dev test, staging, QA,

00:35:33.690 --> 00:35:37.520
UAT in virtual format
only taking a matter

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:38.540
of two to three minutes.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:40.165
And they're all just
using the pointers

00:35:40.165 --> 00:35:42.440
to point back to our
Actifio golden copy,

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:45.620
so they're not consuming
any additional displace.

00:35:45.620 --> 00:35:48.980
So it's great for
Matt and the team.

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:51.110
We were able to help them
replicate from one region

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:53.660
to the other for
multi-terabyte databases

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:56.480
and give them the security
in a disaster scenario

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:59.100
to kind of be back up and
running in a matter of minutes.

00:35:59.100 --> 00:36:01.340
But also for their
DR capabilities,

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:03.180
test that in a
protected environment.

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:05.170
So I'm going to turn
